#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""check-handoff — validate a session's `handoff` receipt block in HANDOFFS.md.

starter-kit/HANDOFFS.md (SEED disposition, see bin/_manifest.py) is a per-session
close-out receipt ledger: one fenced ```handoff block per session, newest on top
(see that file's own "Format" section for the field list and the two write points —
Phase 1B writes a `status: pending` stub, Phase 3D overwrites it to `status:
complete`).

This checker verifies PRESENCE and STRUCTURE only, never semantic quality (that
remains a human judgement call at Phase 3A — see the file's own epigraph: "a green
bin/check-handoff is not a good handoff"). It isolates the newest fenced block and
asserts: every required key is present and non-empty (Session 1 is exempt from
`predecessor_score`); `self_score` / `predecessor_score` are integers 1..10;
`key_files` carries at least one `path:line` token; `what_was_done` names a commit
sha or the literal `pending`; `status` is `complete` or `reconciled` (`pending` is a
finding unless `--allow-pending`); and a handful of anti-pattern lints reject lazy
filler ("pick next from backlog", "need to verify", bare TODO/TBD/"Done."/"Fixed
stuff"/"...").

TWO SCHEMAS, SELECTED BY THE BLOCK'S OWN `status`. A Phase 1B claim stub is a
different artifact from a close-out receipt and is partial by design, so a block with
`status: pending` is graded against STUB_REQUIRED_KEYS — `session`, `date`, `status`,
`active_task` — and the other nine keys are optional-if-absent. Everything else takes
the 13-key close-out schema. The selector is the BLOCK, never the flag: grading by
`--allow-pending` would let a `status: complete` receipt missing nine keys pass.

A stub is a SHORTER receipt, never a SLOPPIER one. Any of the nine that IS present is
checked by every rule that would apply to it at close-out, and a present-but-blank one
is a finding in its own right ("omit the line instead") so that blanking a value never
becomes an escape. Two narrow relaxations, both scoped to stubs: `self_score` and
`predecessor_score` may carry the literal `pending` (unknowable at claim time — see
STUB_SCORE_SENTINEL_KEYS), and conversely the other three floor keys — `session`,
`date`, `active_task` — may NOT, since they are knowable at claim time. `status` is
exempt from that second rule: `pending` is its mandated Phase 1B value, and is what
selects this schema in the first place.

WHAT A STUB CHECK DOES NOT PROVE. The floor is four keys, so a stub whose
`active_task` is a single character still passes; the epigraph above governs here too.
This asserts that a claim was FILED, never that it was filed usefully.

Block isolation matters: a receipt is parsed from the text BETWEEN a
```handoff / ``` fence pair, never by grepping the whole file — prose outside the
fence (including this file's own illustrative example, wrapped in a 4-backtick
outer fence per CommonMark's fence-nesting rule) must never satisfy or trip a check.
The newest receipt is the FIRST ```handoff block in file order, so a documentation
example MUST be wrapped in a stronger (4-backtick) fence — an UNWRAPPED ```handoff
placed above the real receipt would be treated as a real receipt and shadow it.

Part of the close-out-receipt vertical slice (P1: starter-kit/HANDOFFS.md + manifest
wiring; P2: this checker + bin/tests.sh Tests 21-22).

THE ANSWER-SLOT RULE (the `commit:` field, every receipt EXCEPT the newest).
`commit:` is the receipt's join key into git. It may legitimately read `pending`
when written, because a close-out receipt ships INSIDE the very commit whose sha it
would name — the chicken-egg the ratified plan solved by deferring. What the
distributed spec then promises (starter-kit/HANDOFFS.md:64 and :78-79) is that the
NEXT session reconciles it to a real sha. Nothing performed that promise: measured
over both ledger files, 9 of 32 receipts named no sha in the answer slot, one of
them 25 days old, and this checker passed all of them because it reads only the
newest block and `pending` is not a BARE_PLACEHOLDER.

So the rule enforced here is a state predicate over the FILE, not a duty assigned to
a session: the answer slot is the value's FIRST token, and on every receipt except
the newest it must be a sha. The newest is exempt BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by
flag — it is the one receipt that may still be shipping in its own commit — which is
why nothing here can re-create the chicken-egg. This is a leading-token rule on
purpose: it accepts trailing prose (most reconciled receipts carry some), and it
catches the `this commit — ...` dialect that names no sha at all, which keying on
the literal word `pending` would miss.

Scope note, deliberate: this pass iterates blocks[1:], but `validate()` still runs on
blocks[0] ONLY — the 13-key schema is not applied to older receipts. Upstream issue
#65 separately proposes a `--all` mode for this tool covering different ground
(duplicate/malformed row detection across the whole ledger). This is not that, is not
named that, and does not answer it.

THE LOCATOR-FORM RULE (the `changelog_ref` field, EVERY receipt including the newest).
`changelog_ref` points at the session's entry in the action ledger. A `CHANGELOG.md:68`
line number is not a locator into that ledger, it is a POSITIONAL ADDRESS into a file
whose prefix is rewritten on a schedule — so the reference does not go stale in the
ordinary way, it goes WRONG: it keeps resolving, to a real entry, that is not the one
the receipt means. Measured over both ledger files: 9 such anchors in 8 receipts, 8 of
them correct the day they were written, and 0 of 9 resolving to their stated referent
afterwards. Four now land in the file's front matter, above every entry, and four land
inside an entry written on a later day.

The cause is NOT "CHANGELOG.md is prepend-only" — under strict prepending an anchor at
:35 lands on the newest heading forever. It is that the FRONT MATTER is itself edited
in place: this repo's first `### ` heading moved 35 -> 39 -> 68 across two such edits.
Published entries also get rewritten mid-file, and the v3.6 archive split moved 50
entries out of the file entirely. Any one of those breaks a line number; nothing an
author does at write time defends against them.

So the rule is a PROHIBITION, not a correctness assertion: `changelog_ref` may not
contain a root-relative positional address into a live ledger file. Three properties
follow, and each is deliberate:

  * NO blocks[0] EXEMPTION, unlike the answer-slot rule above. That exemption exists
    because `commit:` has a chicken-egg — the receipt ships inside the commit whose sha
    it would name. A prohibition has none: it is satisfiable the instant the receipt is
    written. The corpus also shows write-time values are not self-validating anyway —
    S28's second anchor was wrong the day it was written.
  * THE PREDICATE READS ONLY THE RECEIPT'S OWN BYTES. No prepend, retitle, front-matter
    expansion or archive split can turn a passing receipt red later. The rejected
    alternative — asserting that each reference RESOLVES to a real `### ` heading —
    fails the suite whenever someone legitimately retitles a ledger entry, and this repo
    retitles entries precisely to correct false claims (`de46858`). A guard that
    punishes correcting a false claim gets narrowed, not obeyed (FM #17).
  * PREFIX-AWARE, because three populations look alike and only one is the defect.
    `starter-kit/CHANGELOG.md:92` cites a distributed TEMPLATE, and
    `docs/archive/CHANGELOG-through-v3.6.md:46` cites a shard frozen at write; both are
    stable and both stay legal. Only a bare, root-relative `CHANGELOG.md:<N>` /
    `HANDOFFS.md:<N>` is a finding. Reusing KEY_FILES_RE here would be unsound — it
    matches all three.

Scope is `changelog_ref` ONLY, and that is a smaller claim than the defect. The same
anchors sit in 14 receipts' `key_files` values, where they decay identically — but
`key_files` is REQUIRED by this very checker to carry a `path:line` token, so the two
rules would collide head-on, and one archived receipt's entire `key_files` value is a
single such anchor that cannot be rewritten without inventing a citation. Recorded as a
backlog item, deliberately not bundled (FM #17).

Usage:
  bin/check-handoff                    # check the newest receipt in ./HANDOFFS.md,
                                        # falling back to starter-kit/HANDOFFS.md
  bin/check-handoff --file PATH        # check an explicit file (what tests use)
  bin/check-handoff --allow-none       # "no receipt blocks found" is success (empty-diff session)
  bin/check-handoff --allow-pending    # don't count "status: pending" as a finding
                                        # (that is ALL this flag does — the stub schema
                                        # above is selected by the block, not by this)
  bin/check-handoff --all              # validate EVERY receipt + whole-file structure (issue #65)
  bin/check-handoff --archived         # FILE is a frozen archive shard, not the live ledger

--all exists because the default newest-only scope is a real blind spot: a mangled OLDER
receipt reported green forever, so a corruption could survive a full session and be caught
only by hand-diffing the file across two revisions. It adds the ledger-level invariants —
fences balance, no receipt body stranded outside a fence, `session:`/`date:` lead every
block, no two receipts share a session id AND date (identity is the pair: a ledger may
merge two `S<N>` sequences) — and validates every block against the same schema-selection
logic above (a block's own `status` decides stub vs. full, never the flag), rather than
block 0 alone. `--allow-pending` still narrows to the NEWEST block under --all: a
just-written Phase 1B stub is legitimately pending, an older one never closed out. The
default stays newest-only so the close-out fast path is unchanged.

Exit: 0 = a receipt is present and structurally complete, or legitimately absent
      (fresh seed, or --allow-none with none found), AND every receipt below the
      newest names a commit sha; 1 = missing / structurally incomplete / an
      anti-pattern lint fired / an unreconciled `commit:` answer slot below the newest.
Python 3 stdlib only; cross-platform.
"""
import argparse
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

SEED_SENTINEL = "METHODOLOGY-SEED-SENTINEL"

REQUIRED_KEYS = (
    "session", "date", "status", "self_score", "predecessor_score", "active_task",
    "what_was_done", "next_steps", "key_files", "gotchas", "runtime_smoke",
    "changelog_ref", "commit",
)

# A Phase 1B claim stub is a DIFFERENT artifact from a close-out receipt, and it is
# partial by design: the originating plan says so at
# docs/planning/close-out-receipt-durable-artifact-plan.md:122 ("write the `handoff`
# block with `status: pending` and the fields it can fill now"), ratified twice over by
# its D2/D3, and both distributed specs propagate it faithfully —
# starter-kit/SESSION_RUNNER.md:91 ("the fields you can fill now (`session`, `date`,
# `active_task`)") and starter-kit/HANDOFFS.md:26 ("filling what you can").
#
# So a stub is validated against a SHORTER schema, never a SLOPPIER one: these four
# keys are required, the other nine are optional-if-absent but checked at full strength
# whenever they ARE present. ADDED POLICY, labelled as such: no ratified text ever
# enumerates a stub's keys, and this list departs from the plan's own §5 ("every
# required key present and non-empty", :151), whose exemption list at :163-165 is
# scoped "so it never false-fails a legitimate close-out" and has no stub carve-out.
# The warrant for preferring :122 is that it is ratified by two numbered decisions
# while §5 is plain plan body, already ruled design-guidance rather than binding
# (docs/archive/CHANGELOG-through-v3.6.md:1283-1284).
STUB_REQUIRED_KEYS = ("session", "date", "status", "active_task")

# Inside a stub ONLY, and for these two keys ONLY, the literal `pending` counts as
# absence. `self_score` is unknowable at claim time by construction — it scores work
# not yet done — and the two ledger sequences that share this file evolved two
# spellings for that fact: omit the key (18 of 21 historical stubs) or write `pending`
# (3, all by the framework's author). Both are now legal. This extends a sentinel the
# seed already blesses for two other keys at starter-kit/HANDOFFS.md:77-79
# ("`commit: pending` and `what_was_done: pending` are legal at write time").
STUB_SCORE_SENTINEL_KEYS = ("self_score", "predecessor_score")

KEY_RE = re.compile(r"^(" + "|".join(REQUIRED_KEYS) + r"):\s*(.*)$")
HANDOFF_OPEN_RE = re.compile(r"^```handoff[ \t]*$")

# `key_files` must carry a `path:line` token somewhere in the value (e.g.
# "SessionManager.java:245"). The pre-colon token must be PATH-LIKE — contain a
# "/" or "." — so an incidental colon-digit run in prose (a time "10:30", a ratio
# "3:1", a scripture ref "John 3:16", "auth:2fa") does NOT vacuously satisfy the
# check. Tradeoff: an extension-less top-level file needs a path prefix
# ("./Makefile:12"). This is the "at least one path:line token in the whole value"
# reading, not a stricter per-entry reading (each comma/space-separated entry
# carrying its own token).
KEY_FILES_RE = re.compile(r"[^\s:]*[/.][^\s:]*:\d+")

# A sha-shaped token anywhere in what_was_done; short (7) to full (40) hex, and
# containing at least one hex LETTER so a bare 7+ digit decimal (a count, a unix
# timestamp) does not pass as a "commit sha" — the far more common false-negative.
# (A rare all-numeric short sha is still carried in the separate `commit:` field.)
SHA_RE = re.compile(r"\b(?=[0-9a-f]{7,40}\b)[0-9a-f]*[a-f][0-9a-f]*\b")

PICK_BACKLOG_RE = re.compile(r"pick next(\s+from)?(\s+the)?\s+backlog", re.IGNORECASE)
NEED_TO_VERIFY_RE = re.compile(r"need to verify", re.IGNORECASE)
BARE_PLACEHOLDERS = {"todo", "tbd", "done.", "fixed stuff", "..."}


def scan(text: str):
    """Return (blocks, findings).

    `blocks` is one dict per top-level ```handoff block — {"content", "line"} — in
    file order (first = newest, since the file is newest-on-top). `findings` holds
    LEDGER-level structural defects that only exist between blocks, not within one:
    an unclosed fence, and a receipt body stranded outside every fence.

    Fence-nesting aware: a bare all-backtick line (3+ backticks, no language tag —
    e.g. HANDOFFS.md's own 4-backtick "````" wrapper around its illustrative
    example) opens an inert wrapper that is skipped whole, per CommonMark's
    fence-nesting rule (a fence can only be closed by a fence of >= its own
    backtick count). Without this, a ```handoff shown as documentation INSIDE a
    stronger wrapper would be misparsed as a real receipt.

    The orphan check is what catches the issue-#65 Evidence B corruption: a session
    prepending its receipt reused the previous block's opening fence and overwrote
    the `session:`/`date:` lines under it, leaving that receipt's body as loose
    prose with an unmatched closing fence — which then also scrambles fence-matching
    for the block BELOW it, so one corruption silently damages two receipts.
    """
    lines = text.splitlines()
    blocks = []
    findings = []
    outside = []
    i, n = 0, len(lines)
    while i < n:
        stripped = lines[i].strip()
        if stripped and set(stripped) == {"`"} and len(stripped) >= 3:
            # Bare wrapper fence: skip to its matching (or stronger) closer.
            fence_len = len(stripped)
            open_line = i + 1
            i += 1
            closed = False
            while i < n:
                s2 = lines[i].strip()
                i += 1
                if set(s2) == {"`"} and len(s2) >= fence_len:
                    closed = True
                    break
            if not closed:
                findings.append(
                    "line %d: unclosed ``` fence — opened here, never closed" % open_line
                )
            continue
        if HANDOFF_OPEN_RE.match(lines[i]):
            open_line = i + 1
            i += 1
            content = []
            closed = False
            while i < n:
                s2 = lines[i].strip()
                if set(s2) == {"`"} and len(s2) >= 3:
                    i += 1
                    closed = True
                    break
                content.append(lines[i])
                i += 1
            if not closed:
                findings.append(
                    "line %d: ```handoff block opened here is never closed" % open_line
                )
            blocks.append({"content": "\n".join(content), "line": open_line})
            continue
        outside.append(i + 1)
        i += 1

    for ln in outside:
        if KEY_RE.match(lines[ln - 1]):
            findings.append(
                "line %d: receipt field outside any ```handoff fence — an orphaned "
                "receipt body: %r" % (ln, lines[ln - 1].strip()[:60])
            )

    return blocks, findings


def extract_blocks(text: str) -> list:
    """Inner content of each top-level ```handoff block, in file order."""
    blocks, _findings = scan(text)
    return [b["content"] for b in blocks]


def validate_ledger(blocks: list, findings: list, allow_pending: bool) -> list:
    """Whole-ledger invariants across EVERY receipt, not just the newest.

    `allow_pending` applies ONLY to the newest block: a just-written Phase 1B stub
    is legitimately pending mid-session, but an OLDER receipt left pending is a
    session that never closed out — exactly what this mode exists to surface.
    """
    seen = {}
    for idx, b in enumerate(blocks):
        fields = parse_block(b["content"])
        label = fields.get("session", "").strip() or "<no session>"
        prefix = "block at line %d (%s)" % (b["line"], label)

        order = []
        for line in b["content"].splitlines():
            m = KEY_RE.match(line)
            if m:
                order.append(m.group(1))
        if order[:2] != ["session", "date"]:
            findings.append(
                "%s: first two keys must be `session` then `date` (got %s)"
                % (prefix, ", ".join(order[:2]) or "none")
            )

        for msg in validate(fields, allow_pending and idx == 0):
            findings.append("%s: %s" % (prefix, msg))

        # Identity is session + date, NOT session alone. `S<N>` is a per-sequence
        # counter, and a ledger may legitimately merge more than one sequence — a
        # fork and its upstream each running their own, so two distinct sessions
        # share an `S<N>` by construction. Keying on the id alone asserts an
        # invariant the format in `starter-kit/HANDOFFS.md` never states, and
        # false-positives on such a ledger; a checker that cries wolf on a valid
        # file is how a gate becomes furniture. The realistic corruption this
        # still catches is a block copied and not edited, which duplicates both.
        sid = fields.get("session", "").strip()
        date = fields.get("date", "").strip()
        if sid:
            key = (sid, date)
            if key in seen:
                findings.append(
                    "%s: duplicate receipt identity — session %r on date %r "
                    "(first seen at line %d)" % (prefix, sid, date, seen[key])
                )
            else:
                seen[key] = b["line"]

    return findings


def parse_block(block_text: str) -> dict:
    """key: value pairs found INSIDE an already-isolated block. Only recognized
    keys (REQUIRED_KEYS) are captured, so free-text prose lines never masquerade
    as a field."""
    fields = {}
    for line in block_text.splitlines():
        m = KEY_RE.match(line)
        if m:
            fields[m.group(1)] = m.group(2).strip()
    return fields


def check_int_range(key: str, value: str, lo: int = 1, hi: int = 10):
    try:
        n = int(value.strip())
    except ValueError:
        return "%s must be an integer %d..%d (got %r)" % (key, lo, hi, value)
    if not (lo <= n <= hi):
        return "%s must be an integer %d..%d (got %r)" % (key, lo, hi, value)
    return None


def validate(fields: dict, allow_pending: bool) -> list:
    """Return a list of finding strings; empty list = structurally clean."""
    findings = []
    session = fields.get("session", "").strip()
    is_s1 = session == "S1"

    # THE SCHEMA SELECTOR IS THE BLOCK, NOT THE FLAG. A block is a Phase 1B stub iff it
    # says so itself. Keying this off `allow_pending` instead would be a hole, not a
    # shortcut: a `status: complete` receipt checked with the flag would then be graded
    # against the 4-key schema and pass while missing nine keys. bin/tests.sh Test 24
    # N1 is that exact receipt, and it is the reason this line reads `is_stub`.
    status = fields.get("status", "").strip()
    is_stub = status == "pending"
    required = STUB_REQUIRED_KEYS if is_stub else REQUIRED_KEYS

    def sentinelled(key):
        # The `key in STUB_SCORE_SENTINEL_KEYS` conjunct is DEFENSIVE, not load-bearing,
        # and no test can kill it: the only two call sites below already pass exactly
        # those two key names, so removing it is a behavioural no-op today (mutation-
        # verified — the mutant survived the whole suite). It is kept so that a future
        # third call site cannot silently widen the sentinel to a key that never earned
        # it. Recorded rather than claimed as coverage.
        return (is_stub and key in STUB_SCORE_SENTINEL_KEYS
                and fields.get(key, "").strip() == "pending")

    for key in required:
        if key == "predecessor_score" and is_s1:
            continue  # Session-1 exemption — no predecessor to score
        if key not in fields:
            findings.append("missing required key: %s" % key)
        elif not fields[key].strip():
            findings.append("empty required field: %s" % key)

    if is_stub:
        # "Optional" governs PRESENCE only. A key that is present but blank would
        # otherwise slip past every value check below, making a blanked line a
        # universal escape — so absence is legal and emptiness is not.
        for key in REQUIRED_KEYS:
            if key not in required and key in fields and not fields[key].strip():
                findings.append("empty optional field: %s (omit the line instead)" % key)
        # The floor keys are knowable at claim time — that is why they are the floor —
        # so the `pending` sentinel must not spread to them. `status` is exempt:
        # `pending` is its mandated Phase 1B value.
        for key in STUB_REQUIRED_KEYS:
            if key != "status" and fields.get(key, "").strip().lower() == "pending":
                findings.append(
                    "%s must carry a real value at Phase 1B (it is knowable at claim time)" % key
                )

    # self_score / predecessor_score are distinct keys — each validated on its own
    # value, so one can never stand in for the other.
    if fields.get("self_score", "").strip() and not sentinelled("self_score"):
        msg = check_int_range("self_score", fields["self_score"])
        if msg:
            findings.append(msg)
    if fields.get("predecessor_score", "").strip() and not sentinelled("predecessor_score"):
        msg = check_int_range("predecessor_score", fields["predecessor_score"])
        if msg:
            findings.append(msg)

    if fields.get("key_files", "").strip() and not KEY_FILES_RE.search(fields["key_files"]):
        findings.append(
            "key_files has no path:line token (e.g. SessionManager.java:245): %r" % fields["key_files"]
        )

    wwd = fields.get("what_was_done", "")
    if wwd.strip() and wwd.strip() != "pending" and not SHA_RE.search(wwd):
        findings.append(
            "what_was_done names no commit sha and is not the literal 'pending': %r" % wwd
        )

    # "reconciled" is the terminal status the next session's Phase 0 backfill writes for a
    # receipt it reconstructed after a crash/skip (see the reconcile-on-read backstop); it is a
    # valid closed state, like "complete".
    if is_stub and not allow_pending:
        findings.append("receipt still pending — close-out not completed")
    elif status and status not in ("pending", "complete", "reconciled"):
        findings.append("status must be 'complete', 'reconciled', or 'pending' (got %r)" % status)

    next_steps = fields.get("next_steps", "")
    if PICK_BACKLOG_RE.search(next_steps):
        findings.append("next_steps is a non-actionable placeholder ('pick next from backlog')")

    # Anti-pattern lints applied to every present field's value.
    for key, value in fields.items():
        if NEED_TO_VERIFY_RE.search(value):
            findings.append("%s contains a deferred-verification placeholder ('need to verify')" % key)
        if value.strip().lower() in BARE_PLACEHOLDERS:
            findings.append("%s is a bare placeholder (%r)" % (key, value.strip()))

    return findings


def leads_with_sha(value: str) -> bool:
    """True iff the ANSWER SLOT — the first whitespace-delimited token of the value —
    is a commit sha.

    Backticks, and a trailing comma/semicolon/colon, are stripped before matching:
    receipts write the sha bare (`1298af7 — ...`), fenced (`` `1298af7` ``) and
    punctuated (`7603f10 (extraction...)`) interchangeably, and all three name a sha
    in the slot. An em-dash or "(" immediately following without a space does not
    occur in the corpus and is deliberately NOT stripped — widening this predicate
    should be driven by a real receipt, not by anticipation.

    Reuses SHA_RE, so the same "at least one hex letter" rule applies here as in
    what_was_done: a bare 7+ digit decimal is not a sha. `fullmatch`, not `search` —
    `search` would accept "pending-1298af7" and, worse, accept any prose whose first
    word merely CONTAINS a sha-shaped run.
    """
    token = value.strip().split()[0] if value.strip() else ""
    return bool(SHA_RE.fullmatch(token.strip("`,;:")))


def check_answer_slots(blocks: list, archived: bool = False) -> list:
    """Return findings for every receipt BELOW the newest whose `commit:` answer slot
    names no sha. See THE ANSWER-SLOT RULE in the module docstring.

    blocks[0] is skipped unconditionally and WITHOUT inspecting its value — the
    exemption is positional, not value-based, so no close-out receipt can ever be
    failed for the deferral the spec explicitly permits it. A missing `commit:` key
    below the newest is a finding too: absence is not a pass, or omitting the line
    would be a free escape from a rule that only reads present values.

    `archived=True` removes that exemption, and the reason is a hole this rule had on
    its first run against the real corpus. "Newest" is a property of the LEDGER, not
    of a file. Once the ledger is sharded (this repo's own HANDOFFS.md +
    docs/archive/HANDOFFS-archive.md), the archive's blocks[0] is merely the newest
    receipt IN THAT SHARD — it is not shipping in its own commit and has no claim on
    the exemption. Checking the archive without this flag silently passed S18, which
    had stood unreconciled for three days. An archive shard contains no newest
    receipt, so every block in it is checked.
    """
    findings = []
    for block in (blocks if archived else blocks[1:]):
        fields = parse_block(block)
        # A still-open Phase 1B stub below the newest means a session claimed and never
        # closed out. That is `status: pending`'s finding to raise, not this one's, and
        # Phase 0 reconcile is its remedy — do not double-report it here.
        if fields.get("status", "").strip() == "pending":
            continue
        value = fields.get("commit", "")
        if not leads_with_sha(value):
            findings.append(
                "receipt %s (%s) names no commit sha in its `commit:` answer slot: %r"
                % (fields.get("session", "?"), fields.get("date", "?"), value.strip()[:60])
            )
    return findings


# A ROOT-RELATIVE positional address into a live ledger file. See THE LOCATOR-FORM RULE
# in the module docstring. group(1) is the directory prefix and is the whole point: the
# match is a finding ONLY when that prefix is empty.
#
# Leftmost-match semantics do the prefix capture for us — scanning "starter-kit/CHANGELOG.md:92"
# left to right, the attempt at "s" succeeds with group(1) == "starter-kit/", so the engine
# never reaches the position at "CHANGELOG" where group(1) would be empty. The lookbehind
# stops a match from starting mid-token ("kit/CHANGELOG…" cannot begin at "k").
#
# `[\w.\-]*` after the filename stem keeps the archived shards in scope for the prefix test:
# CHANGELOG-through-v3.6.md and HANDOFFS-archive.md must MATCH here so their non-empty
# prefixes can exempt them. Requiring `:\d+` is what keeps a plain filename mention legal.
LIVE_LEDGER_ANCHOR_RE = re.compile(
    r"(?<![\w.\-])((?:[\w.\-]+/)*)((?:CHANGELOG|HANDOFFS)[\w.\-]*\.md):(\d+)"
)


def check_locator_forms(blocks: list) -> list:
    """Return findings for every receipt whose `changelog_ref` carries a root-relative
    positional address into a live ledger file. See THE LOCATOR-FORM RULE in the module
    docstring.

    EVERY block is checked, blocks[0] included — the answer-slot rule's positional
    exemption does not transfer, because a prohibition has no chicken-egg to solve.

    One finding per ANCHOR, not per receipt, so the count equals the number of tokens a
    repair has to remove; one receipt in the corpus carries two.

    ONE skip, and it is a real guard: a `status: pending` block is a Phase 1B stub whose
    schema makes `changelog_ref` optional-if-absent. Without it this pass would fire on
    every session's own claim commit.

    An absent or empty `changelog_ref` is NOT skipped explicitly, and the omission is
    deliberate. Emptiness is `validate()`'s finding on the newest receipt, and on an older
    one it would assert a 13-key schema this pass does not apply — but a guard for it
    would be unkillable: an empty string contains no anchor, so the pass is already silent
    on it. A line no mutant can falsify is not a guard, it is a comment that looks like
    one. This is that comment, in the place where it is true.
    """
    findings = []
    for block in blocks:
        fields = parse_block(block)
        if fields.get("status", "").strip() == "pending":
            continue
        value = fields.get("changelog_ref", "")
        for match in LIVE_LEDGER_ANCHOR_RE.finditer(value):
            if match.group(1):  # a directory prefix — a template or a frozen shard, not the defect
                continue
            findings.append(
                "changelog_ref in receipt %s (%s) addresses a live ledger by line number: %r"
                % (fields.get("session", "?"), fields.get("date", "?"), match.group(0))
            )
    return findings


def repo_root() -> Path:
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, check=True,
        )
        return Path(out.stdout.strip())
    except (FileNotFoundError, subprocess.CalledProcessError):
        return Path.cwd()


def default_path() -> Path:
    """The repo's own HANDOFFS.md if present, else the starter-kit seed template
    (the only place HANDOFFS.md lives inside the canonical methodology repo, which
    has no root-level receipt ledger of its own)."""
    root = repo_root()
    root_file = root / "HANDOFFS.md"
    if root_file.exists():
        return root_file
    return root / "starter-kit" / "HANDOFFS.md"


def main() -> int:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Validate the newest `handoff` receipt block in a HANDOFFS.md ledger."
    )
    p.add_argument("--file", help="explicit HANDOFFS.md path to check (what bin/tests.sh uses)")
    p.add_argument("--allow-none", action="store_true",
                    help="treat 'no receipt blocks found' as success (empty-diff / no-action session)")
    p.add_argument("--allow-pending", action="store_true",
                    help="do not count 'status: pending' as a finding; this flag changes nothing else")
    p.add_argument("--archived", action="store_true",
                    help="FILE is an archived ledger shard, not the live ledger: no receipt in it "
                         "is the newest, so check every block's `commit:` answer slot (the newest "
                         "receipt's exemption is a property of the LEDGER, not of a file)")
    p.add_argument("--all", action="store_true",
                    help="validate EVERY receipt in the ledger plus whole-file structure "
                         "(issue #65); default stays newest-only for the close-out fast path")
    args = p.parse_args()

    path = Path(args.file) if args.file else default_path()
    if not path.exists():
        print("error: handoff file not found: %s" % path)
        return 1

    text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    blocks_meta, struct_findings = scan(text)
    blocks = [b["content"] for b in blocks_meta]

    if not blocks:
        if SEED_SENTINEL in text:
            print("note: fresh receipt ledger, no receipts yet (%s)" % path)
            return 0
        if args.allow_none:
            print("note: no receipt blocks found in %s (--allow-none)" % path)
            return 0
        print("error: no ```handoff``` receipt block found in %s" % path)
        return 1

    if args.all:
        findings = validate_ledger(blocks_meta, list(struct_findings), args.allow_pending)
        if findings:
            print("check-handoff --all: FAIL — %d issue(s) across %d receipt(s) in %s:"
                  % (len(findings), len(blocks), path))
            for msg in findings:
                print("  error: %s" % msg)
            return 1
        print("check-handoff --all: OK — %d receipt(s) in %s, all structurally complete, "
              "fences balanced, no duplicate session+date." % (len(blocks), path))
        return 0

    fields = parse_block(blocks[0])  # newest = first block in the file
    findings = validate(fields, args.allow_pending)

    # Reported separately from the schema findings above, and computed over a
    # different scope, so the two can never be read as one number: `validate()` is the
    # 13-key schema on the NEWEST receipt; this is one property on every OLDER one.
    anchor_findings = check_answer_slots(blocks, args.archived)

    # A third scope, reported in its own block for the same reason the second one is:
    # `validate()` is the 13-key schema on the NEWEST receipt, `check_answer_slots` is one
    # property on every OLDER one, and this is one property on EVERY one. Three different
    # denominators; never render them as one number.
    #
    # The message must not begin "receipt" — `nslot()` (bin/tests.sh) counts findings with
    # `grep -c '^  error: receipt'`, so a row starting that way would be silently absorbed
    # into the answer-slot tests' assertions.
    locator_findings = check_locator_forms(blocks)

    if findings or anchor_findings or locator_findings:
        if findings:
            print("check-handoff: FAIL — %d issue(s) in the newest receipt in %s:"
                  % (len(findings), path))
            for msg in findings:
                print("  error: %s" % msg)
        if anchor_findings:
            print("check-handoff: FAIL — %d unreconciled `commit:` answer slot(s) in %s:"
                  % (len(anchor_findings), path))
            for msg in anchor_findings:
                print("  error: %s" % msg)
            print("  note: the spec (starter-kit/HANDOFFS.md:64, :78-79) says the next session")
            print("        reconciles `commit:` to a real sha. Find the sha a receipt should name")
            print("        with: git log --all --full-history --format=%H -- <this file>")
            print("        then the first commit whose copy of that block reads `status: complete`.")
        if locator_findings:
            print("check-handoff: FAIL — %d positional ledger address(es) in `changelog_ref` in %s:"
                  % (len(locator_findings), path))
            for msg in locator_findings:
                print("  error: %s" % msg)
            print("  note: a line number into a live ledger is not a locator — the file's own")
            print("        front matter is edited in place and its entries are archived, so the")
            print("        number keeps resolving, to the wrong entry. Name the entry instead:")
            print('        CHANGELOG.md "<its ### heading>". A prefixed path (starter-kit/…,')
            print("        docs/archive/…) is stable and stays legal.")
        return 1

    label = ("a valid Phase 1B stub (close-out not yet performed)"
             if fields.get("status", "").strip() == "pending" else "structurally complete")
    checked = len(blocks) if args.archived else len(blocks) - 1
    print("check-handoff: OK — newest receipt in %s is %s; all %d %sreceipt(s) name a commit sha."
          % (path, label, checked, "" if args.archived else "older "))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
